      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The reservation covers parts of Coconino, Yavapai, and Mohave counties, with Peach Springs as its tribal capital.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hualapai",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hualapai War began in 1865 when increased traffic on the Fort Mojave‑Prescott Toll Road sparked armed conflict.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hualapai",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During that war, roughly one‑third of the Hualapai people died from battle or disease.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hualapai",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hualapai language belongs to the Pai branch of the Yuman–Cochimí family, also spoken by the Havasupai.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
